# The other processors start at boot by themselves; `cpu auto off` keeps
# them waiting from the next boot on (saved in /system/cpu).
@disk
@cpus 4
$ cpu
~ ^processor: \S
count: 4, 4 in use
~ ^clock: \d+ MHz$
~ ^can do:
$ cpu auto off
only one processor at the next boot (saved)
$ cat /system/cpu
auto off
@reboot
$ cpu
~ ^processor: \S
count: 4, 1 in use ('cpu start' starts the others)
~ ^clock: \d+ MHz$
~ ^can do:
$ cpu start
4 of 4 processors in use
$ cpu auto on
the other processors start at boot (saved)
@reboot
$ cpu test
~ ^hashing 64 MiB on 1 processor: \d+ ms$
~ ^hashing 64 MiB on 4: \d+ ms$
~ ^same result; \d+\.\d times as fast$
$ cpu auto sometimes
usage: cpu [start | test | auto on|off]
